Found in k198. Epic's installed-build guide says a blank EngineAssociation is valid for a project inside the engine tree: the editor is found by its location in the directory hierarchy. chooseEngine in internal/config/engine.go instead falls back to the highest installed Launcher engine and never searches upward from the .uproject, so such a project opens in the wrong engine.

Owner: unreagent-worker. Done when: on a blank association the parent directories are searched for an engine root (hasEditor) first, pinned by a test with a temp tree; the existing fallback stays for a project outside any engine tree. Source is documentation only (marked doc in the unreagent-ue skill).

Scout read of the UE 5.8.4 engine source on 2026-11-22 (source only, nothing run; paths relative to the engine root). Confirmed: a blank EngineAssociation is resolved by walking up the parent directories; a parent counts as engine root when it has Engine/Binaries and Engine/Build (Engine/Source/Developer/DesktopPlatform/Private/DesktopPlatformBase.cpp 401-421, 454-498). No fallback to the highest installed engine. Add to this card: a value containing / or \ is a path relative to the project (468-480) — classifyAssociation treats it as a build id today. A non-string JSON value counts as blank. Also: the .uproject may be UTF-8 with or without BOM or UTF-16 LE/BE (Runtime/Core/Private/Misc/FileHelper.cpp 145-185); our reader is UTF-8 only. And: UnrealEditor.exe <uproject> itself never looks at the association (only version selector, UBT, project generation), so the chooseEngine warning 'the editor may offer to convert the project' has no source — soften it.

Linux side takes only the comment correction about project conversion noted above (worked under k202, comment-only). The code change of this card stays with the Windows side.

From the k202 review (UE 5.8.4 source, read not run): the editor does prompt 'Project file is out of date. Would you like to update it?' via GameProjectUtils::CheckForOutOfDateGameProjectFile (called from Engine/Source/Editor/UnrealEd/Private/UnrealEdGlobals.cpp). The trigger is the .uproject FileVersion and plugin references, not EngineAssociation. When softening the chooseEngine warning string (internal/config/engine.go) and the test comment in cmd/launcher/main_test.go, do not claim the editor never prompts. The doc comment of chooseEngine was already corrected under k202.

